<body>

<header>

<h1>📐 Grade 9 Mathematics</h1>

<p>Unit 7 — Congruency and Similarity</p>

<p>Study Plus • Complete Notes</p>

</header>

<div class="container">


<!-- 7.1 -->

<div class="topic">

<h2>7.1 Revision on Congruency and Similarity</h2>

<h3>Congruent Figures</h3>

<p>
Two figures are <b>congruent</b> if they have exactly the same
shape and exactly the same size.
</p>

<div class="formula">
Congruent → Same shape + Same size
</div>

<p>
The symbol for congruency is:
</p>

<div class="formula">
≅
</div>

<div class="example">

If triangle ABC and triangle DEF are congruent:

<br><br>

△ABC ≅ △DEF

<br><br>

Corresponding sides and corresponding angles are equal.

</div>

<h3>Similarity</h3>

<p>
Two figures are similar if they have the same shape but
their sizes may be different.
</p>

<div class="formula">
Similar → Same shape + Corresponding sides proportional
</div>

<div class="example">

A small triangle and a larger triangle can be similar
even though their sizes are different.

</div>

<div class="trick">

⚡ Remember:

<br><br>

Congruent = same shape + same size

<br>

Similar = same shape, size may be different.

</div>

</div>


<!-- 7.2 -->

<div class="topic">

<h2>7.2 Definition of Similar Figures</h2>

<p>
Two plane figures are called <b>similar figures</b> when:
</p>

<ul>

<li>Corresponding angles are equal.</li>

<li>Corresponding sides are proportional.</li>

</ul>

<div class="formula">

Corresponding angles are equal

<br><br>

Corresponding sides have the same ratio.

</div>

<h3>Scale Factor</h3>

<p>
The scale factor tells us how much one figure has been enlarged
or reduced compared with another figure.
</p>

<div class="formula">

Scale Factor =
Corresponding side of image ÷ Corresponding side of original

</div>

<div class="example">

A triangle has a side of 4 cm.

A similar triangle has the corresponding side 8 cm.

<br><br>

Scale factor = 8 ÷ 4 = 2

<br><br>

Therefore the second triangle is twice as large.

</div>

<div class="important">

If the scale factor is:

<br><br>

<b>k > 1</b> → enlargement

<br>

<b>0 < k < 1</b> → reduction

<br>

<b>k = 1</b> → same size

</div>

</div>


<!-- 7.3 -->

<div class="topic">

<h2>7.3 Theorems on Similar Plane Figures</h2>

<h3>Similarity Theorem</h3>

<p>
If two figures have equal corresponding angles and proportional
corresponding sides, then the figures are similar.
</p>

<div class="formula">

Corresponding angles equal

<br><br>

Corresponding sides proportional

<br><br>

Therefore → Similar figures

</div>


<h3>SSS Similarity Theorem</h3>

<p>
SSS means <b>Side-Side-Side</b>.
</p>

<p>
Two triangles are similar if their three corresponding sides
are proportional.
</p>

<div class="formula">

AB/DE = BC/EF = AC/DF

<br><br>

Therefore:

△ABC ~ △DEF

</div>

<div class="example">

Triangle ABC has sides:

<br>

3 cm, 4 cm, 5 cm

<br><br>

Triangle DEF has sides:

<br>

6 cm, 8 cm, 10 cm

<br><br>

Ratios:

<br>

6/3 = 2

<br>

8/4 = 2

<br>

10/5 = 2

<br><br>

All ratios are equal.

<br><br>

Therefore the triangles are similar by SSS.

</div>


<h3>SAS Similarity Theorem</h3>

<p>
SAS means <b>Side-Angle-Side</b>.
</p>

<p>
Two triangles are similar if two corresponding sides are
proportional and the included angle is equal.
</p>

<div class="formula">

AB/DE = AC/DF

<br><br>

∠A = ∠D

<br><br>

Therefore:

△ABC ~ △DEF

</div>

<div class="example">

If:

<br>

AB/DE = AC/DF = 2

<br>

and

<br>

∠A = ∠D

<br><br>

then the two triangles are similar by SAS.

</div>

<div class="trick">

🎯 Exam Trick:

<br><br>

SSS → compare 3 sides.

<br>

SAS → compare 2 sides + included angle.

</div>

</div>


<!-- 7.4 -->

<div class="topic">

<h2>7.4 Ratio of Perimeters of Similar Plane Figures</h2>

<p>
For similar figures, the ratio of their perimeters is equal
to the ratio of their corresponding sides.
</p>

<div class="formula">

P₁ / P₂ = k

</div>

<p>
where <b>k</b> is the scale factor.
</p>

<div class="example">

Two similar triangles have corresponding sides:

<br><br>

4 cm and 8 cm.

<br><br>

Scale factor:

<br>

8/4 = 2

<br><br>

Therefore the ratio of their perimeters is:

<br>

P₂/P₁ = 2

</div>

<div class="important">

⭐ Important:

<br><br>

Length ratio = Perimeter ratio

</div>

</div>


<!-- 7.5 -->

<div class="topic">

<h2>7.5 Ratio of Areas of Similar Plane Figures</h2>

<p>
For similar figures, the ratio of their areas is the square
of the ratio of their corresponding sides.
</p>

<div class="formula">

Area ratio = k²

</div>

<div class="example">

Two similar figures have a scale factor of 3.

<br><br>

Area ratio:

<br>

3² = 9

<br><br>

Therefore the larger figure has 9 times the area.

</div>

<div class="important">

⭐ Remember:

<br><br>

Side ratio = k

<br>

Perimeter ratio = k

<br>

Area ratio = k²

</div>

<div class="trick">

⚡ Very Important Exam Trick:

<br><br>

If length doubles:

<br>

Perimeter → doubles

<br>

Area → becomes 4 times

</div>

</div>


<!-- 7.6 -->

<div class="topic">

<h2>7.6 Construction of Similar Figures</h2>

<p>
Similar figures can be constructed by enlarging or reducing
a given figure using a fixed scale factor.
</p>

<h3>Steps</h3>

<ol>

<li>Draw the original figure.</li>

<li>Choose the required scale factor.</li>

<li>Multiply every corresponding length by the scale factor.</li>

<li>Construct the new figure using the new lengths.</li>

<li>Make sure corresponding angles remain equal.</li>

</ol>

<div class="example">

Suppose a triangle has sides:

<br><br>

3 cm, 4 cm and 5 cm.

<br><br>

If scale factor = 2:

<br><br>

New sides:

<br>

3 × 2 = 6 cm

<br>

4 × 2 = 8 cm

<br>

5 × 2 = 10 cm

<br><br>

The new triangle is similar to the original.

</div>

<div class="warning">

⚠️ When constructing similar figures, all corresponding
lengths must be multiplied by the same scale factor.

</div>

</div>


<!-- 7.7 -->

<div class="topic">

<h2>7.7 Applications of Similarity</h2>

<p>
Similarity is useful for finding unknown lengths and heights
that are difficult to measure directly.
</p>

<h3>Finding Unknown Lengths</h3>

<div class="formula">

Corresponding sides must have equal ratios.

<br><br>

a/b = c/d

</div>

<div class="example">

A small triangle has:

<br><br>

Height = 3 m

Base = 4 m

<br><br>

A similar triangle has base = 12 m.

Find its height.

<br><br>

3/4 = h/12

<br><br>

4h = 36

<br><br>

h = 9 m

</div>


<h3>Using Shadows</h3>

<p>
Similar triangles can be used to find the height of an object
using its shadow.
</p>

<div class="example">

A person is 2 m tall and has a shadow of 3 m.

A tree has a shadow of 12 m.

<br><br>

Using similarity:

<br><br>

2/3 = h/12

<br><br>

3h = 24

<br><br>

h = 8 m

<br><br>

Therefore the tree is 8 m tall.

</div>


<h3>Maps and Models</h3>

<p>
Maps and models use scale factors to represent large objects
or distances using smaller measurements.
</p>

<div class="example">

If a map scale is:

<br><br>

1 cm = 5 km

<br><br>

Then 4 cm on the map represents:

<br><br>

4 × 5 = 20 km

</div>


<div class="important">

🎯 Real-life applications of similarity include:

<br><br>

• Measuring height of buildings

<br>

• Measuring height of trees

<br>

• Maps

<br>

• Models

<br>

• Engineering drawings

<br>

• Architecture

<br>

• Photography

</div>

</div>


<!-- SUMMARY -->

<div class="topic">

<h2>⭐ Unit 7 Quick Summary</h2>

<div class="formula">

Congruent → Same shape + Same size

<br><br>

Similar → Same shape + Proportional sides

<br><br>

SSS Similarity → 3 proportional sides

<br><br>

SAS Similarity → 2 proportional sides + included angle

<br><br>

Perimeter ratio = k

<br><br>

Area ratio = k²

</div>

<div class="trick">

🎯 Exam Memory Trick:

<br><br>

<b>Length → k</b>

<br>

<b>Perimeter → k</b>

<br>

<b>Area → k²</b>

<br><br>

SSS → 3 sides

<br>

SAS → 2 sides + angle

</div>

</div>


